systemd: degrade SCMP_ACT_KILL_PROCESS to SCMP_ACT_KILL below libseccomp API 3

Stage-2 attempt 8 is the first boot that reaches a usable system: 4 failed units
instead of attempt 7's 9, step NAMESPACE down from 20 to 0, and getty and adbd
both up.  dhcpcd.service is one of the four that remain, and it is the only one
that fails for this reason:

  dhcpcd.service: Main process exited, code=exited, status=228/SECCOMP

228 is EXIT_SECCOMP, so the unit never ran at all -- seccomp_load() failed and
the child bailed before exec.  SCMP_ACT_KILL_PROCESS is SECCOMP_RET_KILL_PROCESS
in the kernel, which is Linux 4.14 and later.  This kernel is 4.9.190, so
/proc/sys/kernel/seccomp/actions_avail does not exist, libseccomp cannot find the
action listed as supported, and the whole filter is rejected.

systemd used to handle exactly this.  scmp_act_kill_process() sat in
seccomp-util.c and returned SCMP_ACT_KILL when the API level was too low; it was
dropped once the minimum supported kernel moved past 4.14.  This restores it and
puts the two remaining callers back on it.

Note dhcpcd's unit does not ask to be killed.  It sets SystemCallFilter= without
SystemCallErrorNumber=, and src/core/execute.c initialises .syscall_errno to
SECCOMP_ERROR_NUMBER_KILL, so the kill action is systemd's default for any unit
that filters syscalls without naming an errno.  This was confirmed on the device
by bisecting with systemd-run: a transient unit with SystemCallFilter=@system-service
and no SystemCallErrorNumber= fails 228 identically, and the same unit with
SystemCallErrorNumber=EPERM starts.  dhcpcd is simply the only unit in this
system's closure that takes the default -- journald, udevd, logind and timesyncd
all set errno=1 -- which is why one service and not twenty is affected.

The degradation is not lossless: SCMP_ACT_KILL is SECCOMP_RET_KILL_THREAD, so a
violating thread dies rather than the process.  That is what every systemd before
the removal did on kernels this old, and it is strictly better than a filter that
does not load, which leaves the unit dead and unsandboxed.

dlopen_libseccomp() is called from inside the helper rather than assumed: the
exec-invoke.c caller runs before any other seccomp entry point has dlopen'd the
library, so sym_seccomp_api_get would still be NULL there.  It is idempotent, and
a failure to load means seccomp is unusable anyway, so that path answers with the
conservative action.
--- a/src/shared/seccomp-util.h
+++ b/src/shared/seccomp-util.h
@@ -28,6 +28,8 @@
